# Network Configuration Documentation

This is the actual Cisco IOS configuration behind the enterprise network project — the commands I ran to build each piece, plus notes on why.

> IPs and interface numbers below match the final topology. If you're adapting this for your own lab, your interface numbering will probably be different depending on the devices you're using.

---

## 1. VLANs

Created one VLAN per department plus a couple for infrastructure:

```cisco
vlan 10
 name IT

vlan 20
 name HR

vlan 30
 name ACCOUNTING

vlan 40
 name SERVER

vlan 99
 name MANAGEMENT

vlan 999
 name NATIVE
```

## 2. Access Ports

End-device ports get assigned to their VLAN with PortFast on, since these are edge ports and there's no reason to wait through the normal STP listening/learning delay:

```cisco
interface fa0/1
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
 spanning-tree portfast
```

Repeated this pattern for the rest of the IT endpoints and the other departments' ports.

## 3. Trunking

Infrastructure links between switches are 802.1Q trunks:

```cisco
interface gigabitEthernet0/1
 switchport mode trunk
```

Only the VLANs that actually need to cross each trunk are allowed, and VLAN 999 is set as the native VLAN instead of leaving it on VLAN 1:

```cisco
interface gigabitEthernet0/1
 switchport trunk native vlan 999
 switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30,40,99,999
 switchport mode trunk
```

## 4. Multilayer Switch SVIs

Both multilayer switches get an SVI per VLAN, giving each one its own Layer 3 address to route with.

**MSW1:**

```cisco
interface vlan 10
 ip address 10.10.10.1 255.255.255.224
 no shutdown

interface vlan 20
 ip address 10.10.20.1 255.255.255.240
 no shutdown

interface vlan 30
 ip address 10.10.30.1 255.255.255.224
 no shutdown

interface vlan 40
 ip address 10.10.40.1 255.255.255.240
 no shutdown

interface vlan 99
 ip address 10.10.99.1 255.255.255.240
 no shutdown
```

**MSW2:**

```cisco
interface vlan 10
 ip address 10.10.10.2 255.255.255.224
 no shutdown

interface vlan 20
 ip address 10.10.20.2 255.255.255.240
 no shutdown

interface vlan 30
 ip address 10.10.30.2 255.255.255.224
 no shutdown

interface vlan 40
 ip address 10.10.40.2 255.255.255.240
 no shutdown

interface vlan 99
 ip address 10.10.99.2 255.255.255.240
 no shutdown
```

And obviously nothing routes until you actually turn routing on:

```cisco
ip routing
```

## 5. HSRP

Virtual gateway addresses, one per VLAN:

```
VLAN 10 → 10.10.10.3
VLAN 20 → 10.10.20.3
VLAN 30 → 10.10.30.3
VLAN 40 → 10.10.40.3
VLAN 99 → 10.10.99.3
```

**MSW1** gets bumped to priority 110 (with preempt) on VLANs 10, 30, and 99 so it wins the active role for those:

```cisco
interface vlan 10
 standby 10 ip 10.10.10.3
 standby 10 priority 110
 standby 10 preempt
```

```cisco
interface vlan 30
 standby 30 ip 10.10.30.3
 standby 30 priority 110
 standby 30 preempt
```

```cisco
interface vlan 99
 standby 99 ip 10.10.99.3
 standby 99 priority 110
 standby 99 preempt
```

It just sits at the default priority of 100 for VLANs 20 and 40, letting MSW2 win those instead.

**MSW2** gets the same treatment in reverse — priority 110 on VLANs 20 and 40:

```cisco
interface vlan 20
 standby 20 ip 10.10.20.3
 standby 20 priority 110
 standby 20 preempt
```

```cisco
interface vlan 40
 standby 40 ip 10.10.40.3
 standby 40 priority 110
 standby 40 preempt
```

End result: the active gateway role is split across both switches instead of piling onto one.

## 6. EtherChannel

LACP bundle between the two multilayer switches, two physical links each:

**MSW1:**

```cisco
interface range gigabitEthernet1/0/23-24
 channel-group 1 mode active
```

Which shows up as:

```cisco
interface port-channel1
```

**MSW2** gets the identical config:

```cisco
interface range gigabitEthernet1/0/23-24
 channel-group 1 mode active
```

Both ends use LACP active mode so they actually negotiate instead of just hoping the config matches. To check it bundled correctly:

```cisco
show etherchannel summary
```

You want to see `Po1` with both physical interfaces showing as bundled members.

## 7. Rapid PVST+

```cisco
spanning-tree mode rapid-pvst
```

PortFast and BPDU Guard on edge-facing interfaces:

```cisco
spanning-tree portfast
spanning-tree bpduguard enable
```

And globally, so I don't have to remember to add it on every new edge port:

```cisco
spanning-tree portfast default
spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default
```

Checked with:

```cisco
show spanning-tree
```

## 8. OSPF

Running OSPF process 1, with each multilayer switch advertising its directly connected networks:

```cisco
router ospf 1
 router-id 2.2.2.2
 network 10.10.10.0 0.0.0.31 area 0
 network 10.10.20.0 0.0.0.15 area 0
 network 10.10.30.0 0.0.0.31 area 0
 network 10.10.40.0 0.0.0.15 area 0
 network 10.10.99.0 0.0.0.15 area 0
```

The other multilayer switch runs the same config with its own router ID.

R1 uses router ID `1.1.1.1`, backed by a loopback so its identity in OSPF doesn't depend on any single physical link staying up:

```cisco
interface loopback1
 ip address 1.1.1.1 255.255.255.255
```

To confirm adjacencies actually formed and routes are showing up:

```cisco
show ip ospf neighbor
show ip route ospf
```

Neighbors should reach `FULL` state — if they're stuck at `2-WAY` or `EXSTART`, something's off with MTU or network type, not just a timing issue.

## 9. OSPF Passive Interfaces

Worth calling out since it tripped me up initially: `passive-interface` doesn't stop a network from being advertised — it just stops that interface from sending hellos and forming a neighbor relationship. Useful on VLAN interfaces facing end hosts, where there's no reason to try to form an OSPF adjacency with a workstation.

```cisco
router ospf 1
 passive-interface vlan 10
```

## 10. DHCP

One pool per VLAN. IT, for example:

```cisco
ip dhcp pool IT
 network 10.10.10.0 255.255.255.224
 default-router 10.10.10.3
```

And HR:

```cisco
ip dhcp pool HR
 network 10.10.20.0 255.255.255.240
 default-router 10.10.20.3
```

Same pattern for the rest of the VLANs. Excluded the address range used by the SVIs and HSRP virtual IP so DHCP doesn't try to hand those out to a client:

```cisco
ip dhcp excluded-address 10.10.10.1 10.10.10.3
```

Checked assignments with:

```cisco
show ip dhcp binding
show ip dhcp pool
```

## 11. Extended ACLs

Blocking HR ↔ Accounting in both directions, permitting everything else:

**HR side:**

```cisco
ip access-list extended HR-ACL
 deny ip 10.10.20.0 0.0.0.15 10.10.30.0 0.0.0.31
 permit ip any any
```

**Accounting side:**

```cisco
ip access-list extended ACCOUNTING-ACL
 deny ip 10.10.30.0 0.0.0.31 10.10.20.0 0.0.0.15
 permit ip any any
```

Applied to the relevant SVIs. `show access-lists` is useful here beyond just confirming the rules exist — the match counters tell you whether traffic is actually hitting the deny lines, which is how I caught a couple of early misconfigurations.

## 12. SSH

Needs a hostname and domain name before RSA keys will generate:

```cisco
hostname MSW1
ip domain-name enterprise.local
```

```cisco
crypto key generate rsa
```

Local admin account:

```cisco
username admin privilege 15 secret <password>
```

VTY lines locked to SSH only — no Telnet fallback:

```cisco
line vty 0 4
 login local
 transport input ssh
```

Forced SSHv2:

```cisco
ip ssh version 2
```

Confirmed with `show ip ssh`, then tested from an actual host:

```
ssh -l admin <device-ip>
```

## 13. Management VLAN

VLAN 99 (`10.10.99.0/28`, gateway `10.10.99.3`) is dedicated to device management traffic, kept separate from anything departmental, and carried across the trunks that need it.

## 14. Native VLAN

VLAN 999 is a dummy native VLAN with nothing actually on it — just there so trunk ports aren't defaulting to VLAN 1 as native, which is a common and pretty avoidable weak spot.

```cisco
vlan 999
 name NATIVE
```

```cisco
switchport trunk native vlan 999
```

## 15. Port Security

On access ports, locked down to one MAC address with a shutdown violation action:

```cisco
interface fa0/1
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
 switchport port-security
 switchport port-security maximum 1
 switchport port-security violation shutdown
```

Checked with `show port-security`.

